title: Scattered Spider / MGM
page: breaches/scattered-spider-mgm.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: One help desk call of about 10 minutes gave Scattered Spider (UNC3944) initial access to MGM; ALPHV claims Okta super administrator access and ransomware on 100+ ESXi hypervisors followed.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: $100M | or more in estimated losses | a separate $45M settlement covered its 2019 and 2023 breaches
stat: 10 | days of operational disruption | beginning with a 36+ hour initial outage
stat: 100+ | ESXi hypervisors encrypted | by ALPHV's count
It began with one call of about 10 minutes to the IT help desk. The page names the threat actor as Scattered Spider (UNC3944), working with ALPHV/BlackCat through a ransomware-as-a-service affiliate relationship.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with, and the call where it began.

The page puts MGM's estimated losses at more than $100 million in its headline figures, and at about $100 million in the stage detail. Separately, MGM later agreed to a $45 million settlement of class actions over its 2019 and 2023 data breaches, so that figure covers more than this attack. The page says MGM refused to pay the ransom.

Operations were disrupted for 10 days, beginning with an initial outage of more than 36 hours. The page ties that outage to MGM's own shutdown of its systems and the encryption that followed. By ALPHV's count, more than 100 VMware ESXi hypervisors were encrypted with ALPHV/BlackCat ransomware. A hypervisor is the software that runs virtual machines, and a later slide explains it.

It began with one call to MGM's IT help desk, lasting about 10 minutes. The page says the entire initial access chain required no technical exploit whatsoever.

The page names the threat actor as Scattered Spider (UNC3944), working with ALPHV/BlackCat through a ransomware-as-a-service affiliate relationship. RaaS, ransomware as a service, is a model in which affiliates deploy a ransomware operation's software.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: September 2023 | MGM compromised, starting with one help desk call of about 10 minutes
event: Before deployment | Data exfiltrated; MGM shuts down its systems, and ALPHV says no ransomware came first
event: September 11, 2023 | Ransomware on 100+ ESXi hypervisors, by ALPHV's account, after a day's wait
event: Over 10 days | Disruption across Las Vegas properties, beginning with a 36+ hour initial outage
The 100+ count, the date and the day's wait are ALPHV's account. The outage covers MGM's shutdown and the encryption after it. The page dates the call only to September 2023. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page gives two dates, and the timeline shows only those.

The page says Scattered Spider compromised MGM Resorts International in September 2023, starting with a single phone call to the IT help desk that lasted about 10 minutes. It gives no exact day for the call.

Before deploying ransomware, the page says, the attacker exfiltrated data to use as leverage. MGM shut down its systems to mitigate risk to customer information, and ALPHV says no ransomware was deployed before that shutdown. The page does not say whether the theft or the shutdown came first, so they share one marker.

By ALPHV's account, after waiting a day and trying but failing to get in touch with MGM, the attackers launched ransomware against more than 100 ESXi hypervisors on September 11, 2023. With the shutdown and the encryption that followed, the initial outage lasted more than 36 hours, and casino floor operations, hotel check-ins, digital room keys, ATMs and slot machines were disrupted for 10 days across multiple Las Vegas properties.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

== Who said what, and about whom
layout: compare
col: The page, on MGM | One help desk call, about 10 minutes | MGM shut down its systems | About $100M in estimated losses
col: ALPHV's claims | Okta and Azure AD admin privileges | 100+ ESXi hypervisors encrypted | Still had access; threatened more attacks
col: Reports on the group | Mandiant: PII held, new-phone pretext | Okta: a second IdP, customers unnamed | Microsoft: Tenant Root Group scope
The third column's reports cover the group, or Okta's customers, generally, without naming MGM.
notes:
The page draws on three kinds of source, and they carry different weight.

The first column is what the page states in its own voice about MGM: the help desk call of about 10 minutes and the MFA reset, MGM's shutdown of its systems, 10 days of disruption, losses of about $100 million, and MGM's refusal to pay.

The second column is ALPHV's own account. ALPHV claimed super administrator privileges in MGM's Okta and Global Administrator privileges in its Azure AD tenant, and says it launched ransomware against more than 100 ESXi hypervisors on September 11, 2023. It also claimed to still have access to MGM infrastructure and threatened further attacks. The page also quotes vx-underground on X, relaying ALPHV: "All ALPHV ransomware group did to compromise MGM Resorts was hop on LinkedIn, find an employee, then call the Help Desk."

The third column is reporting on the group, or on Okta's customers, in general. Mandiant reported on UNC3944 across multiple engagements without naming MGM. Okta's August 2023 advisory names no customer. Microsoft's October 2023 report on the group as Octo Tempest names no victims. CrowdStrike's January 2023 BYOVD finding covers Scattered Spider generally.

When you present a stage, keep each detail's attribution.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Reconnaissance | LinkedIn | T1591
step: 02 | Initial Access | Help desk call | T1566.004
step: 03 | Internal Reconnaissance | SharePoint docs | T1213.002
step: 04 | Credential & Identity Access | Okta, per ALPHV | T1078.004
step: 05 | Cloud Privilege Escalation | Azure AD and VMs | T1578.002
step: 06 | Lateral Movement & Persistence | Tools on hand | T1021.001
step: 07 | Exfiltration | Data theft | T1657
step: 08 | Impact | ESXi, per ALPHV | T1486
Several stages rest on ALPHV's claims or on reports about the group generally.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's eight stages, each with its phase and an ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stage 01 is reconnaissance on LinkedIn. Stage 02 is the call to the help desk and the MFA reset. Stage 03 is internal reconnaissance in SharePoint documentation. Stage 04 is Okta Super Administrator access, by ALPHV's account. Stage 05 is Azure AD, where ALPHV claimed Global Administrator privileges, with new virtual machines as a foothold in Mandiant's reporting on the group. Stage 06 is lateral movement and persistence with tools already present in the environment. Stage 07 is data theft before encryption, and stage 08 is the ransomware ALPHV says hit more than 100 ESXi hypervisors.

The page's summary gives this order for MGM: LinkedIn research, the call and the MFA reset, and then, as ALPHV claimed, Okta and Azure AD privileges, lateral movement and encryption. Several stages also carry detail that Mandiant, Okta, Microsoft or CrowdStrike reported about the group, or about Okta's customers, in general. The stage slides keep those labels.

The next slides take each stage in turn.

== An MGM employee, researched on LinkedIn
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Reconnaissance
technique: T1591 | Gather Victim Org Information
technique: T1589.002 | Gather Victim Identity Information: Email Addresses
fact: Source | LinkedIn: full name, job title, department, manager name
fact: Aim | to impersonate the employee to an IT help desk agent
fact: Mandiant, generally | UNC3944 callers held SSN last 4 digits, birth dates, manager names
fact: Language | Scattered Spider are native English speakers, the page says
Help desks are trained to be helpful; suspicion of an "employee" feels obstructive.
notes:
Stage 01 happened before any call. The page maps it to T1591, Gather Victim Org Information, and T1589.002, Email Addresses.

The attacker used LinkedIn to identify an MGM Resorts employee and gather their full name, job title, and enough personal and professional detail to impersonate them convincingly to an IT help desk agent. OSINT, in the page's phase label, is open-source intelligence: information gathered from public sources.

Mandiant, reporting on UNC3944 across multiple engagements without naming MGM, noted from forensic recordings of the group's help-desk calls that callers already held their targets' PII, including SSN last four digits, dates of birth and manager names, to pass standard help desk identity verification. Mandiant points to the extensive research performed on potential victims and the high success rate.

The page adds that Scattered Spider are native English speakers, which removes the accent barrier that typically flags social engineering attempts from non-Western threat actors.

The line at the bottom of the slide is the page's reason the call worked: help desks are trained to be helpful, so suspicion of an employee feels obstructive.

== One call of about 10 minutes got an MFA reset
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1566.004 | Phishing: Spearphishing Voice
technique: T1684.001 | Social Engineering: Impersonation
fact: Mandiant, generally | UNC3944's consistent pretext: a new phone needing an MFA reset
fact: Verification | the PII given matched what the attacker had gathered
fact: Missing | phishing-resistant, out-of-band identity verification
fact: Skill needed | zero technical skill for this step, the page says
The agent could not verify the caller beyond the PII provided, the page says.
notes:
Stage 02 is the call. The page maps it to T1566.004, Phishing: Spearphishing Voice, and T1684.001, Social Engineering: Impersonation. Vishing is voice phishing: phishing by telephone.

The attacker called MGM's IT help desk, impersonated the employee identified on LinkedIn, and requested a multi-factor authentication reset. The call lasted approximately 10 minutes.

Mandiant reported that across various UNC3944 victims the consistent pretext was a claim to be receiving a new phone, a routine scenario that naturally requires an MFA reset. Keep that attribution: it describes the group's habit, reported without naming MGM.

The agent had no way to verify the caller's true identity beyond the PII provided, which matched what the attacker had gathered. The page names the gap: the help desk had no phishing-resistant, out-of-band identity verification. Out-of-band means through a separate channel from the request itself.

The page calls this step zero technical skill. It also quotes vx-underground on X, relaying ALPHV: "All ALPHV ransomware group did to compromise MGM Resorts was hop on LinkedIn, find an employee, then call the Help Desk."

== Reading first: SharePoint as a roadmap
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Internal Reconnaissance
technique: T1213.002 | Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint
technique: T1046 | Network Service Discovery
fact: Platform | SharePoint, the internal intranet and documentation portal
fact: Mandiant, generally | UNC3944 frequently targeted VPN, VDI and telework guides
fact: Result | a roadmap of the environment, from the victim's own documents
fact: Detection gap | a recently reset account's SharePoint searches: virtually indistinguishable from legitimate onboarding
Internal IT docs hold network topology, tool names and access paths, the page says.
notes:
Stage 03 is reading. The page maps it to T1213.002, Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint, and T1046, Network Service Discovery.

The page says that with initial account access, the attacker's first move was to read rather than to escalate immediately. Mandiant has reported that in UNC3944 intrusions the group frequently targeted victims' internal SharePoint sites for help guides and documentation covering VPNs, virtual desktop infrastructure, or VDI, and remote telework utilities. A VPN is a virtual private network.

The page says that gave a roadmap of the environment drawn entirely from the victim's own internal documentation, dramatically accelerating lateral movement planning without triggering any security tooling.

The page also names a detection gap. SharePoint search activity by a recently reset account is virtually indistinguishable from legitimate onboarding.

No defense on the page is aimed at this stage. That comes back in the second discussion question.

== Okta Super Administrator, by ALPHV's account
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Credential & Identity Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
technique: T1098 | Account Manipulation
fact: Okta | identity provider for MGM's whole enterprise application estate
fact: Mandiant, generally | UNC3944 self-assigned an account to every app in Okta
fact: Okta, August 2023 | unnamed customers: a second IdP, and in some cases MFA removed
fact: CISA and FBI | list adding a federated IdP among Scattered Spider's techniques
Okta Super Administrator means control of every app federated to it, the page says.
notes:
Stage 04 is the identity provider. The page maps it to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts, and T1098, Account Manipulation.

With initial account access, the attacker escalated to Okta Super Administrator, according to ALPHV, which says it "continued having super administrator privileges to their Okta". Okta was the identity provider for MGM's entire enterprise application estate, and Super Administrator means full control over identity for the applications downstream of it.

Mandiant has reported that in UNC3944 intrusions the group used Okta's self-assignment feature to assign the compromised account to every application in the Okta instance. That gave single sign-on access to every federated application at once, and a visual inventory of every app tile in the Okta portal.

Okta's advisory of August 31, 2023, on social engineering attacks against multiple US-based Okta customers it does not name, describes the attacker configuring a second identity provider to act as an "impersonation app" and, in some cases, removing second factor requirements from authentication policies. Okta published it before MGM disclosed its own incident, so keep it about Okta's customers generally. CISA and the FBI list adding a federated identity provider to the target's SSO tenant among Scattered Spider's techniques.

The next slide explains identity providers, single sign-on and federation in general terms.

== What an identity provider, SSO and federation are
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, IdP | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-idp
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entries for SSO and federation (csoh.org)
An identity provider, or IdP, is the system that authenticates users. Single sign-on means signing in once and getting into many applications without re-entering credentials. Federation is trusting tokens from a foreign IdP.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The glossary defines an identity provider, or IdP, as the system that authenticates users. Okta and Azure AD, now called Microsoft Entra ID, are among the examples it gives.

Single sign-on, or SSO, is signing in once and getting into many applications without re-entering credentials.

Federation is trusting tokens from a foreign IdP. The glossary's example is AWS trusting tokens issued to GitHub Actions, so a workflow can assume a role.

Put together, these explain the page's phrase "every application federated to it": the applications that rely on that identity provider to sign their users in.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-sso and https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-federation

== Azure AD Global Administrator, per ALPHV
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Cloud Privilege Escalation
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
technique: T1578.002 | Modify Cloud Compute Infrastructure: Create Cloud Instance
fact: ALPHV claimed | Global Administrator privileges in MGM's Azure AD tenant
fact: Microsoft | the group, no victims named: User Access Administrator for Tenant Root Group scope
fact: Mandiant, generally | UNC3944 built new VMs via SSO, Defender and telemetry removed
Malicious traffic from inside was indistinguishable from legitimate, the page says.
notes:
Stage 05 moves into Azure. The page maps it to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts, and T1578.002, Create Cloud Instance.

Having compromised Okta, the attacker pivoted to MGM's Azure AD tenant, where ALPHV claimed Global Administrator privileges. Microsoft, in its October 2023 report on the group as Octo Tempest, which names no victims, describes the group assigning the User Access Administrator role to grant Tenant Root Group management scope. The next slide explains the Tenant Root Group in general terms.

Mandiant has reported that in UNC3944 intrusions the group used what Mandiant calls "a more aggressive method of persistence": it accessed vSphere and Azure through SSO applications to create entirely new virtual machines, and conducted all follow-on activity from them. vSphere is VMware's virtualization platform. Those attacker-controlled VMs had Microsoft Defender and Windows telemetry disabled, making forensic investigation significantly harder. The page names MAS_AIO and privacy-script.bat as the tools used to remove them.

The page also lists a PCUnlocker ISO, a disk image, attached to existing VMs through vCenter to reset local admin passwords, bypassing domain controls. vCenter is VMware's management console.

The page's point is that cloud activity sourced from inside the environment made malicious traffic indistinguishable from legitimate traffic. No defense on the page is aimed at this stage.

== What the Tenant Root Group is
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, management groups overview | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/governance/management-groups/overview
Each Azure directory has one top-level management group, the root, shown by default as Tenant root group. Every management group and subscription folds up to it, and access or policy assigned there applies to all resources in the directory.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Azure management groups provide a governance scope above subscriptions. Conditions applied to a management group cascade by inheritance to the subscriptions under it.

Each directory has a single top-level management group called the root management group. By default its display name is Tenant root group, and its ID is the Microsoft Entra tenant ID. It cannot be moved or deleted, and new subscriptions default to it.

Microsoft says any assignment of user access or policy on the root management group applies to all resources within the directory, and that assignments at this scope should be "must have" only.

No one has default access to the root management group. Microsoft says Entra Global Administrators are the only users who can elevate themselves to gain access, and can then assign any Azure role to others. The page gives Tenant Root Group management scope as Microsoft's finding about the group in general. It does not connect ALPHV's Global Administrator claim to this elevation, and neither does this deck.

Azure AD is the earlier name of Microsoft Entra ID.

== Lateral movement with tools already present
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Lateral Movement & Persistence
technique: T1021.001 | Remote Services: Remote Desktop Protocol
technique: T1685 | Disable or Modify Tools
fact: Mandiant, generally | UNC3944 ran whoami and quser via CrowdStrike Real Time Response
fact: Mandiant, generally | UNC3944 used Mimikatz, ADRecon and IMPACKET from attacker VMs
fact: Tunnels, generally | Mandiant: NGROK, RSOCX, Localtonet, Tailscale; Remmina per GuidePoint, on a suspected actor
fact: CrowdStrike, 2023 | BYOVD on the group generally: a CVE-2015-2291 Intel driver
In Mandiant's reporting, the victim's own EDR became a remote access tool.
notes:
Stage 06 is lateral movement. The page maps it to T1021.001, Remote Desktop Protocol, and T1685, Disable or Modify Tools.

With domain-level cloud access, the attacker moved laterally using legitimate tools already present in the environment. LOTL, in the page's phase label, means living off the land: using legitimate tools already in the environment to evade detection.

Mandiant has reported UNC3944 intrusions in which the group created API keys inside CrowdStrike's external console and ran commands such as whoami and quser through its Real Time Response module, using the victim's own EDR, endpoint detection and response, as a remote access tool. The group also used Mimikatz, ADRecon and IMPACKET from attacker-controlled VMs, with tunnelling tools for persistent command and control. Mandiant also lists SaaS applications the group reached through Okta SSO.

The page gives the tunnelling and remote access tools for UNC3944 generally, each with its source: NGROK, RSOCX and Localtonet from Mandiant; Tailscale from Mandiant and GuidePoint GRIT; Remmina from GuidePoint GRIT, reporting on a suspected Scattered Spider actor. It also names a PowerShell script called SecretServerSecretStealer among the credential theft tools.

For EDR evasion, the page cites CrowdStrike's January 2023 reporting on Scattered Spider generally: BYOVD, a CVE-2015-2291 Intel driver used to disable endpoint security agents. The next slide explains BYOVD in general terms.

== What bringing your own vulnerable driver means
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1068 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068/
credit: Also from ATT&CK T1685, Disable or Modify Tools (attack.mitre.org)
ATT&CK says adversaries may bring a signed vulnerable driver onto a compromised machine and exploit it to run code in kernel mode. This is sometimes called Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver, or BYOVD.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

A driver is software that lets the operating system work with a device. Kernel mode is the operating system's most privileged mode, where its core runs.

Under Exploitation for Privilege Escalation, T1068, ATT&CK says adversaries may bring a signed vulnerable driver onto a compromised machine so that they can exploit the vulnerability to execute code in kernel mode. This process is sometimes referred to as Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver. The driver may arrive with files delivered during initial access, or be downloaded to the machine later.

Under Disable or Modify Tools, T1685, ATT&CK describes adversaries disabling, degrading or tampering with security tools such as EDR to reduce visibility. Among its more advanced techniques it lists leveraging legitimate drivers to render tools non-functional.

The page maps its stage 06 to T1685. ATT&CK gives the BYOVD name under T1068.

Also from ATT&CK T1685: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/

== Data taken first, for double extortion
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
technique: T1530 | Data from Cloud Storage
fact: Data targeted | customer PII, loyalty programme data, internal credentials
fact: Method | legitimate cloud storage and remote access tools
fact: ALPHV claimed | it still had access to MGM; threatened further attacks
fact: Caesars | a similar attack at the same time; reportedly paid about $15M
The threat to publish was independent of recovery from backups.
notes:
Stage 07 is the data theft. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft, and T1530, Data from Cloud Storage.

Before deploying ransomware, the attacker exfiltrated sensitive data from MGM's environment, which established the leverage for double extortion: encrypt the systems, and also threaten to leak the data. The page lists the data targeted as customer PII, loyalty programme data and internal credentials, taken with legitimate cloud storage and remote access tools, with no custom malware required.

The attacker threatened to publish the stolen data unless the ransom was paid, independent of whether MGM could recover from encryption using backups. ALPHV also claimed to still have access to MGM infrastructure and threatened further attacks.

The page sets Caesars Entertainment beside MGM. Caesars, hit in a similar attack at the same time, reportedly paid about $15 million to prevent data publication; the figure comes from a Wall Street Journal report, and Caesars' own filing gives no amount. The page says MGM refused and incurred about $100 million in losses instead. It does not say who attacked Caesars.

No defense on the page is aimed at this stage. The second discussion question comes back to it.

== 100+ ESXi hypervisors encrypted, per ALPHV
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Impact
technique: T1486 | Data Encrypted for Impact
technique: T1490 | Inhibit System Recovery
fact: MGM's shutdown | systems shut down; ALPHV says no ransomware came before that
fact: ALPHV's account | 100+ ESXi hypervisors on September 11, 2023, after a day's wait
fact: Disrupted | casino floors, check-ins, room keys, ATMs, slot machines
fact: Losses | an estimated $100M
MGM refused to pay the ransom and incurred the full remediation cost instead.
notes:
Stage 08 is the encryption. The page maps it to T1486, Data Encrypted for Impact, and T1490, Inhibit System Recovery.

MGM shut down its systems to mitigate risk to customer information, and ALPHV says no ransomware was deployed before that shutdown. By ALPHV's account, after waiting a day and trying but failing to get in touch with MGM, the attackers launched ransomware against more than 100 ESXi hypervisors running MGM's production virtual machines on September 11, 2023. The page says the ransomware was deployed through a ransomware-as-a-service affiliate relationship with Scattered Spider.

With the shutdown and the encryption that followed, the initial outage lasted more than 36 hours, and casino floor operations, hotel check-ins, digital room keys, ATMs and slot machines were disrupted for 10 days across multiple Las Vegas properties.

The page says MGM refused to pay and puts its losses at about $100 million. MGM later agreed to a $45 million settlement of class actions over its 2019 and 2023 data breaches.

Morphisec suggests the speed of the encryption and the downtime potentially indicate a network that was not segmented very well and lacked good backup and restoration practices. Keep the word potentially, and Morphisec's name. Two of the page's defenses name this stage. The next slide explains hypervisors in general terms.

== Hypervisors, and ransomware that encrypts their VMs
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1486 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1486/
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry for hypervisor (csoh.org)
A hypervisor is software that creates and isolates virtual machines. ESXi is a Type 1 hypervisor, which runs on bare metal. ATT&CK notes that adversaries may also encrypt virtual machines hosted on ESXi or other hypervisors.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary and MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

The glossary defines a hypervisor as software that creates and isolates virtual machines. A Type 1 hypervisor, such as ESXi, Xen or KVM, runs on bare metal, directly on the server hardware. A Type 2 hypervisor, such as VirtualBox or Parallels, runs on a host operating system.

ATT&CK describes Data Encrypted for Impact as adversaries encrypting data on target systems, or on large numbers of systems in a network, to interrupt availability. With ransomware, the aim is to extract payment in exchange for decryption or a decryption key. ATT&CK adds that adversaries may also encrypt virtual machines hosted on ESXi or other hypervisors.

The page maps its stage 08 to this technique.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-hypervisor

== No exploit needed: one agent following standard procedure
layout: text
The page says the entire initial access chain required one help desk agent following standard procedure, and no technical exploit.
- Lateral movement used legitimate tools already present.
- Exfiltration needed no custom malware.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws.

The page says the entire initial access chain required no technical exploit whatsoever. In its first defense it puts it plainly: the attack required one help desk agent following standard procedure.

The rest of the chain leaned on what was already there. The attacker moved laterally using legitimate tools already present in the environment, and exfiltration used legitimate cloud storage and remote access tools, with no custom malware required.

The page calls the help desk social engineering, Okta and cloud scenario now the most documented and replicated attack chain in enterprise security, and says CISA and the FBI have both published guidance on it. CISA is the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency.

The defenses that follow run from the help desk to the backups, then to rehearsal.

== Seven defenses against eight stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07 | 08
row: Phishing-resistant MFA, no MFA resets by phone | 02
row: Okta treated as Tier 0 | 04
row: Alerts on new IdPs and Org2Org changes | 04
row: ESXi segmented from the identity plane | 08
row: Tunnelling tools detected in odd places | 06
row: Offline, immutable ESXi backups | 08
row: Tabletops for help desk, Okta and cloud | 02 | 04
No defense's instruction is aimed at stage 01, 03, 05 or 07.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's seven defenses against its eight stages. A cell is marked only where a defense acts on something only that stage has.

Phishing-resistant MFA with no phone resets acts on the help desk's MFA reset, stage 02. The page says this single control breaks the entire initial access chain, but its instructions act on the call, not the LinkedIn research, so column 01 is not marked.

Treating Okta as Tier 0 acts on Okta Super Administrator access, new identity provider configuration and admin authenticator resets, all at stage 04. Alerting on Org2Org and new IdP changes acts on stage 04's second identity provider, which the page gives as Okta's account of attacks on its customers generally.

ESXi segmentation acts on the hypervisors, stage 08. It names corporate Okta and Azure AD only as the identity plane to keep ESXi away from, so columns 04 and 05 are not marked for it. Offline backups also act on stage 08's encryption.

Detecting tunnelling tools acts on ngrok, Tailscale and Remmina, stage 06.

The tabletop scenario names help desk social engineering, stage 02, and Okta, stage 04. It ends in "cloud", a word several stages share, so no other column is marked.

Columns 01, 03, 05 and 07 are empty. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Stop phone MFA resets; treat Okta as Tier 0
layout: compare
col: Phishing-resistant MFA (02) | FIDO2 or passkeys, and no help desk MFA resets by phone | Resets move to an out-of-band workflow with manager approval | Visual identity verification: a video call with government ID
col: Okta as Tier 0 (04) | Treat it the way you treat Active Directory | Hardware security keys for all Okta admin access | Okta ThreatInsight on; alerts on new IdPs and admin authenticator resets
Of the first, the page says: this single control breaks the entire initial access chain.
notes:
Two defenses for the first half of the chain.

First, implement phishing-resistant MFA, FIDO2 or passkeys, and never allow help desk agents to reset MFA by phone. The page says this single control breaks the entire initial access chain, and that the attack required one help desk agent following standard procedure, with no technical exploit. Move MFA resets to an out-of-band workflow that requires manager approval and visual identity verification: a video call with government ID. FIDO2 and passkeys are public-key sign-in standards that resist phishing.

Second, treat Okta as a Tier 0 asset, the same way you treat Active Directory. Tier 0 is the most sensitive tier: the assets that control identity for the whole environment. Super Administrator access in Okta gives an attacker control over every application federated to it. Require hardware security keys for all Okta admin access, enable Okta ThreatInsight, and alert immediately on new identity provider configuration or authenticator resets for admin accounts.

== Watch for new IdPs; keep ESXi off the identity plane
layout: compare
col: New IdPs and Org2Org changes (04) | Monitor and alert on Okta Org2Org federation changes | New IdP additions need change control approval and immediate SOC review | Almost never legitimate outside planned migrations, the page says
col: ESXi segmented (08) | Not reachable from identities in the plane of corporate Okta and Azure AD | Separate management networks; dedicated credentials not linked to SSO | Jump hosts with hardware MFA, as the minimum bar
In the attacks on Okta customers that Okta described in August 2023, the attacker configured a second Identity Provider to access applications on behalf of other users.
notes:
Two defenses that watch the identity provider and wall off the hypervisors.

First, monitor and alert on Okta Org2Org federation changes and new identity provider configurations. Org2Org is Okta's integration for connecting one Okta organization to another. In the attacks on Okta customers that Okta described in August 2023, the page says, the attacker configured a second identity provider to access applications on behalf of other users. New IdP additions should require change control approval and trigger immediate SOC review; the page says they are almost never legitimate outside of planned migrations. A SOC is a security operations center.

Second, segment your ESXi environment from the corporate identity plane. ESXi hypervisors should not be reachable from identities that live in the same plane as corporate Okta and Azure AD. The page sets the minimum bar for hypervisor access as separate management networks, dedicated credentials not linked to SSO, and jump hosts with hardware MFA. A jump host is a hardened machine administrators pass through to reach the systems behind it.

== Detect tunnelling tools; keep backups offline
layout: compare
col: Tunnelling tools in odd places (06) | ngrok, Tailscale and Remmina are legitimate tools | Their presence on servers, or in a 2am SOC alert, is not | Detect them on non-developer endpoints; flag mesh VPN clients outside MDM
col: Offline, immutable backups (08) | Of ESXi VM configurations and snapshots | Out of reach of domain credentials | The last line of defence at the hypervisor layer
Morphisec suggests the encryption and downtime potentially indicate a network not segmented very well, lacking good backup and restoration practices.
notes:
Two defenses for the later stages.

First, detect living-off-the-land tools in unusual contexts. The page says ngrok, Tailscale and Remmina are legitimate tools, but their presence on server infrastructure or in a SOC alert at 2am is not. Build detection rules for tunnelling tools on non-developer endpoints, and alert on new VPN mesh clients enrolled outside your MDM. MDM is mobile device management, the system that enrolls and manages an organization's devices. The page attributes ngrok and Tailscale to Mandiant's reporting on UNC3944, Tailscale also to GuidePoint GRIT, and Remmina to GuidePoint GRIT, reporting on a suspected Scattered Spider actor.

Second, keep an offline, immutable backup of ESXi VM configurations and snapshots. An immutable backup cannot be modified or deleted before its retention window expires. Morphisec suggests that the rapid encryption of 100 ESXi servers and more than 36 hours of downtime "potentially indicates a network which was not segmented very well and lacked good backup and restoration practices." Offline backups that cannot be reached with domain credentials are, in the page's words, the last line of defence when ransomware hits the hypervisor layer.

Backups answer the encryption. Stage 07's threat to publish, the page says, was independent of recovery from backups.

== Rehearse the help desk, Okta and cloud scenario
layout: text
Run tabletop exercises for help desk social engineering, then Okta, then cloud; CISA and the FBI have published guidance on it.
- If your incident response plan lacks a playbook for identity provider compromise as an initial step, update it today.
notes:
The last defense is practice.

Run tabletop exercises specifically for the help desk social engineering, Okta and cloud scenario. A tabletop exercise is a discussion-based rehearsal in which a team walks through how it would respond to a scenario. The page calls this now the most documented and replicated attack chain in enterprise security, and says CISA and the FBI have both published guidance on it. The FBI and CISA joint advisory AA23-320A is among the page's sources.

If your incident response plan does not include a playbook for identity provider compromise as an initial step, the page says to update it today.

The scenario names stage 02's help desk call and stage 04's Okta, and the matrix marks those two. The checklist that follows turns all seven defenses into questions.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can a caller get an MFA reset from your help desk by phone?
check: Do all Okta admins sign in with hardware security keys?
check: Would a new identity provider or Org2Org change reach your SOC at once?
check: Can identities in your corporate Okta or Azure AD reach an ESXi host?
check: Would ngrok, Tailscale or Remmina on a server raise an alert?
check: Can domain credentials reach your ESXi backups and snapshots?
check: Does your incident response plan cover identity provider compromise as an initial step?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether a caller could get an MFA reset from your help desk by phone, and whether resets need manager approval and a video call with government ID. Ask whether every Okta admin signs in with a hardware security key, and whether a new identity provider or an Org2Org federation change would reach your SOC immediately.

Ask whether any identity in your corporate Okta or Azure AD can reach an ESXi host, and whether ngrok, Tailscale or Remmina appearing on a server would raise an alert.

Ask whether domain credentials can reach your ESXi backups and snapshots. Finally, ask whether your incident response plan includes a playbook for identity provider compromise as an initial step.

== What does your help desk do with this call?
layout: question
Mandiant reports UNC3944 callers claiming a new phone and holding SSN last 4 digits, birth dates and manager names. A caller like that asks your help desk for an MFA reset.

What happens next, step by step?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

The details come from Mandiant's reporting on UNC3944 across engagements, which does not name MGM: a consistent new-phone pretext, and callers who already held their targets' SSN last four digits, dates of birth and manager names. At MGM, the page says, the agent had no way to verify the caller beyond the PII provided, and one call of about 10 minutes ended in an MFA reset.

Ask each person to walk through what their own help desk would do, and where the process would stop the caller, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says to do.

== What the page says to do about phone resets
layout: answer
The page says never let help desk agents reset MFA by phone.
- Move resets to an out-of-band workflow with manager approval.
- Verify identity visually: a video call with government ID.
- Use phishing-resistant MFA: FIDO2 or passkeys.
notes:
The page's answer is to take the phone out of the reset.

Implement phishing-resistant MFA, FIDO2 or passkeys, and never allow help desk agents to reset MFA via phone. Move MFA resets to an out-of-band workflow requiring manager approval and visual identity verification, a video call with government ID. The page says this single control breaks the entire initial access chain.

Bring the group's walk-throughs back to this. If a step in someone's process accepts answers such as an SSN's last digits, a birth date or a manager's name, Mandiant's reporting shows callers can arrive already holding them.

== What would you add for stages 01, 03, 05 and 07?
layout: question
No defense on the page is aimed at the LinkedIn research, the SharePoint reading, the new VMs, or the data theft.

Pick one stage. What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the defense matrix.

The page's defenses are aimed at stages 02, 04, 06 and 08. None of their instructions is aimed at stage 01, the LinkedIn research; stage 03, the SharePoint documentation reading; stage 05, the Global Administrator access ALPHV claimed in Azure AD and the new virtual machines (the ESXi defense names Azure AD only as the identity plane to keep ESXi away from); or stage 07, the data theft before encryption.

Stage 07 deserves a closer look. The page says the threat to publish was independent of whether MGM could recover from encryption using backups, so backups alone do not remove that leverage.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The matrix leaves these four stages empty
layout: answer
The matrix marks no defense at stages 01, 03, 05 or 07, and this deck adds none. Any control you propose is your own. Test it against reported details, such as SharePoint searches by a recently reset account.
notes:
Be clear that the matrix stops here. It marks no defense at these four stages, and this deck adds none of its own.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports.

For stage 01, those are the LinkedIn details: full name, job title, department and manager name. For stage 03, the SharePoint guides to VPNs, VDI and remote telework utilities that Mandiant reports UNC3944 targeting, and the page's point that searches by a recently reset account look virtually indistinguishable from legitimate onboarding. For stage 05, ALPHV's claim of Global Administrator privileges in MGM's Azure AD tenant, and the new virtual machines with Defender and Windows telemetry removed that Mandiant reports for UNC3944 generally. For stage 07, customer PII, loyalty programme data and internal credentials, taken with legitimate cloud storage and remote access tools, and a threat to publish that was independent of recovery from backups.

